\section{Class Imbalance and How to Handle It}

\subsection*{Before We Begin}
In a classification problem each observation belongs to a \emph{class}, such as “disease” vs. “no disease”.  
Often one class appears many more times than the other; this is called the class frequency.  
Many learning algorithms assume that the classes are roughly equally represented, because they try to treat errors on each class the same way.  
When one class dominates, the algorithm can become biased toward always predicting the majority class.  
To use a model that works well for the rare class, we need special strategies that rebalance the data.

\subsection*{Motivation}
Class imbalance is like a tug‑of‑war where one side has many more players than the other; the heavier side will always pull the rope unless you give the lighter side extra weight or reduce the number of players on the heavy side.  
The problem exists because a model trained on an imbalanced set may ignore the minority class, leading to poor detection of the events we often care most about (e.g., a rare disease).  

\subsection*{Intuition}
If the data are heavily skewed, the learning algorithm sees mostly majority examples and learns to predict that class.  
By adding more minority examples (or by reducing majority examples) we give the learner a more even view of both sides of the problem.  
Techniques such as oversampling, undersampling, data augmentation, SMOTE, and cost‑sensitive learning each change the training data or the learning objective so that the minority class is taken into account more seriously.

\subsection*{Formal Definition}
Let  

\[
N_{\text{maj}} = \text{Number of majority‑class samples},
\qquad
N_{\text{min}} = \text{Number of minority‑class samples}.
\]

When \(N_{\text{maj}} \gg N_{\text{min}}\) we say the dataset is \emph{imbalanced}.

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Oversampling} creates additional minority‑class examples until the numbers become comparable, often by simply duplicating existing samples.
  \item \textbf{Undersampling} randomly discards majority‑class examples so that the remaining count matches the minority side.
  \item \textbf{Data augmentation} applies small, label‑preserving transformations (e.g., rotations of images) to existing minority samples to obtain new, slightly different examples.
  \item \textbf{SMOTE} (Synthetic Minority Over‑sampling Technique) generates synthetic minority points by interpolating between a minority sample and its nearest minority neighbours; the new points are plausible but not exact copies.
  \item \textbf{Cost‑sensitive classifiers} modify the learning objective by assigning a larger penalty to mistakes on the minority class.  Denote this penalty by  

  \[
  w_{\text{min}} = \text{Weight or cost assigned to minority‑class errors}.
  \]

  The algorithm then tries to minimise a loss that heavily discourages misclassifying minority instances.
\end{itemize}

When using K‑Fold cross‑validation, these rebalancing steps must be performed **only** on the training folds; the validation and test folds are left untouched so that performance is measured on the natural, imbalanced distribution.

\subsection*{Worked Example}
\paragraph{Problem statement}
A binary dataset contains 200 samples: 180 belong to the majority class (class 0) and 20 belong to the minority class (class 1).  
We compare three training pipelines:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item[(a)] Use the raw imbalanced data.
  \item[(b)] Oversample the minority class until it also contains 180 samples.
  \item[(c)] Apply SMOTE to generate 160 synthetic minority samples, giving a total of 180 minority examples.
\end{enumerate}

For each pipeline we train a logistic‑regression classifier and evaluate it on an untouched test set of 40 samples (30 majority, 10 minority).  We report accuracy, precision, recall, and F1‑score.

\paragraph{Step 1: Split data}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Randomly hold out 40 samples (30 majority, 10 minority) as the test set; the remaining 160 samples form the training pool.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Step 2: Prepare training data}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item[(a)] \textbf{Raw data}: 144 majority, 16 minority remain in the training pool.
  \item[(b)] \textbf{Oversampling}: replicate the 16 minority examples until there are 144 of them (add 128 copies).  Training set now has 144 majority + 144 minority = 288 samples.
  \item[(c)] \textbf{SMOTE}: for each minority example find its nearest minority neighbours and create 9 synthetic points per original example, producing \(16 + 9\times16 = 160\) synthetic minorities.  Combine with the 144 majority samples → 304 training samples (144 majority, 160 minority).
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{Step 3: Train logistic regression}
Fit a logistic‑regression model on each prepared training set using the same regularisation parameters.

\paragraph{Step 4: Predict on the test set}
The models yield the following confusion matrices on the 40‑sample test set.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{l|cc}
\textbf{Pipeline} & \textbf{Predicted 1} & \textbf{Predicted 0}\\\hline
(a) Raw & TP=6, FP=4 & FN=4, TN=26\\
(b) Oversampling & TP=8, FP=6 & FN=2, TN=24\\
(c) SMOTE & TP=9, FP=7 & FN=1, TN=23\\
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\paragraph{Step 5: Compute metrics}
For each pipeline we calculate  

\[
\text{Accuracy}= \frac{TP+TN}{\text{total}},\qquad
\text{Precision}= \frac{TP}{TP+FP},\qquad
\text{Recall}= \frac{TP}{TP+FN},\qquad
\text{F1}= \frac{2\cdot \text{Precision}\cdot\text{Recall}}{\text{Precision}+\text{Recall}}.
\]

\begin{enumerate}
  \item[(a)] \textbf{Raw data}
  \[
  \text{Accuracy}= \frac{6+26}{40}=0.80,\;
  \text{Precision}= \frac{6}{6+4}=0.60,\;
  \text{Recall}= \frac{6}{6+4}=0.60,\;
  \text{F1}=0.60.
  \]
  \item[(b)] \textbf{Oversampling}
  \[
  \text{Accuracy}= \frac{8+24}{40}=0.80,\;
  \text{Precision}= \frac{8}{8+6}\approx0.57,\;
  \text{Recall}= \frac{8}{8+2}=0.80,\;
  \text{F1}= \frac{2(0.57)(0.80)}{0.57+0.80}\approx0.67.
  \]
  \item[(c)] \textbf{SMOTE}
  \[
  \text{Accuracy}= \frac{9+23}{40}=0.80,\;
  \text{Precision}= \frac{9}{9+7}\approx0.56,\;
  \text{Recall}= \frac{9}{9+1}=0.90,\;
  \text{F1}= \frac{2(0.56)(0.90)}{0.56+0.90}\approx0.69.
  \]
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{What this shows us}
All three models achieve the same overall accuracy because the test set remains imbalanced, but the balanced‑data pipelines (oversampling and SMOTE) substantially improve recall for the minority class, leading to higher F1‑scores.  SMOTE gives the best recall because the synthetic samples are more diverse than simple copies.

\subsection*{Key Takeaway}
Balancing techniques such as oversampling, SMOTE, or cost‑sensitive weighting must be applied only to training folds so that evaluation reflects performance on the true, imbalanced distribution.

